prompt Pick up the running thread and take the next agent-doable lap.
rota lap 4508, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes; its dual seat, growing a language, states the method as five moves whose fifth is live in it as first resident and gate, and its cardinal seat says an infusion is tested by running it a SECOND time. Both point at the same gesture, and this lap took it literally: I ran every parked artifact before believing a word its log said about itself.
think Reds first. `stash_record` read `unlanded=3` -- three Pheromone records in this checkout's dead-letter box, two of them with their code beside them. REDS %636 names the only two closing moves for a parked lap, and one of them is agent-doable: land the record with the work.
think Water tasted first at the corpus rather than the box. `tools/g/glow_run.rish` proves a desk lowers, builds and exits 0 and never reads what the program printed -- so I ran two desks bare and read the lowered Rye. `bound-tick.rye` ends `return if (tick == actual_steps) 0 else 1`, so the exit code IS the assertion and the desks self-check. No red; an honest answer that only running produced. `glow_desk_reach` reads `uncovered=0` now, so %532's ratchet is worked down and only its custody question stands.
think Took stash@{1} rather than stash@{2}: one guard family against two laps' worth, and the crux is Lindy -- the predicate deciding which road a `|%` source lowers down outlives every desk that travels it. Recovered with `git checkout stash@{1} -- <paths>`, a tree read, so the control landed 100755 (`exec-bit`); a blob read would have landed it 100644 and the pen's `sh` invocation would have hidden that.
think Then I proved it on metal rather than reading the parked log's own GREEN, which is the discipline the last recovery from this seat wrote down. Control 18 checks 0 failures in 22.3s; witness exit 0 at 17.7s.
obs THE FINDING THE PARKED LAP MADE: `assert selftest.out contains "GREEN"` over a delegate whose every one of seventeen leg lines ends `-- GREEN` matched the FIRST leg, so the closing summary could be renamed or deleted whole and the guard still read green. Its fifth plant refused nothing, which is how a vacuous leg is caught.
obs THE FINDING RUNNING IT PRODUCED: the same shape stands at the file's own top level. The seventeen-leg `GREEN:` is printed BEFORE the control leg runs, so a run abandoned during the control's 22 seconds leaves a transcript ending on the announcement, byte-identical to a finished one. The sibling `tools/g/glow_desk_reach_witness.rish` already closes on a claim naming its control; this guard does now too. The pen's runner is cut at the CONTROL LEG marker, so the pen's baseline still reads the seventeen-leg claim unchanged.
obs AGAINST MYSELF, and it is the lap's real subject. I lowered `standing_equipment_redleg`'s ceiling 53 -> 52, having measured the population both ways on metal first -- 53 with HEAD's witness, which names this guard in `list`, and 52 with the recovered pair. Then I read the parked log and found an `obs` line saying that lap had declined the same move on purpose, because lowering with no slack re-arms the fleet-wide refusal %670 measured the day before, on a question Keaton holds open. Reverted; it stands at 53 with the reading at 52.
obs So the lap is a measurement of why `stash_record` gates the RECORD rather than the code. The parked files carried the repair; only the parked log carried the reason a correct-looking further step was wrong. A record is the half of the work that holds what a diff cannot.
obs The marker reading is a TEXT reading: I proved on metal that moving the control file aside leaves the count at 52, because the marker `redleg` looks for is the `_control.sh` invocation in the witness, never the control's existence. Nothing is wrong -- an invocation naming an absent file reds the witness at runtime -- and it is worth knowing that this meter's evidence is a name.
obs Row `20260909.211344` was booked `%673` from a local read and never committed; the anointed spine has since published `%673` for a peer's row, so it derives to `%683` after `git fetch xy`. It landed straight onto its own shelf rather than the pin: REDS holds 39,994 of 40,960 and fourteen of its sixteen rows read OPEN, so the two foldable ones belong to peers who left them unclaimed. Second lap running from this seat to land a closed row shelf-first, which makes it a pattern rather than an accident.
obs The parked log's four `file` fields all ship in this commit, so `log_file_claim` has no quarrel with it. One line was rewritten and the rewrite is declared inside the log under `RECOVERED AND ADJUSTED`, the form this seat used last lap -- the record whole, plus a dated addition, never a silent edit.
obs Inserting the recovered log's shelf row put the `20260909` shelf at 105 while both rosters read 104; `session_roster_agree` named it `stale=2` and both were corrected off the shelf's own count rather than typed. The same scan shows two other logs on that shelf carrying no row at all -- 106 logs, 104 rows before this lap. Named, not swept.
obs The cold roster pass was launched at the open and VOIDED by my own hand: I edited the tree while it ran, so its digest reading was worthless. Stopped with `fleet_call --pattern ... --signal TERM`, which refused all seven peer trees by name. Seven concurrent roster passes stood on this pier at the time.
